<script>
const topics = [
{title:"1. What Is a Computer Network?",tag:"Fundamentals",summary:"A network connects computers and other devices so they can communicate and share resources.",definition:"A computer network is a set of computers sharing resources through network nodes and using common communication protocols over digital interconnections.",points:["A network contains two or more connected devices.","Connections may use wired, optical or wireless technologies.","Devices communicate using agreed protocols.","Networks can be arranged in different topologies."],remember:"Think: connected devices + communication rules + shared resources."},
{title:"2. Components of a Computer Network",tag:"Fundamentals",summary:"The basic components work together to connect devices and enable communication.",definition:"The slides identify computers, cables, a Network Interface Card (NIC) on each computer, switches and network operating-system software as network components.",points:["Computers are the devices participating in the network.","Cables or other media provide links between devices.","A NIC provides a computer with a network interface.","Switches connect network devices.","Network software supports network operation."],remember:"Memorize the five items listed in the lecture."},
{title:"3. Benefits of Computer Networks",tag:"Fundamentals",summary:"Networks improve communication, resource use and administration.",definition:"The listed benefits are increased speed, reduced cost, improved security, centralized software management, electronic mail and flexible access.",points:["Increase speed of communication and data exchange.","Reduce cost by sharing resources.","Support improved security.","Allow centralized software management.","Provide email and flexible access."],remember:"Learn all six benefits as a list."},
{title:"4. Server and Client",tag:"Devices",summary:"A client requests a service; a server provides it.",definition:"A server is a device or program that provides functions or services. A client is hardware or software that requests access to a service provided by a server.",points:["A client may be a desktop, laptop, smartphone or browser.","A server may provide files, databases, web pages or shared resources.","Client-server communication follows a request-and-service model."],remember:"Client = requests. Server = provides."},
{title:"5. Switch and Router",tag:"Devices",summary:"A switch connects network devices; a router directs packets toward their destination.",definition:"A switch is a network device whose responsibility is to connect network devices. A router is a device that directs packets or data to their destination.",points:["A switch is used to connect devices within a network.","A router forwards packets toward their destination.","Do not confuse connecting devices with directing packets."],remember:"Switch = connect. Router = route/direct packets."},
{title:"6. Point-to-Point Connection",tag:"Connection Types",summary:"A point-to-point network connects two nodes through a shared communication medium.",definition:"A point-to-point network is a way of connecting two nodes through a common medium, which may be a wired cable or wireless satellite link.",points:["The two nodes may be computers, laptops, mobile devices, routers, hubs or switches.","The medium can be wired or wireless.","The lecture gives an infrared remote-control and television example."],remember:"Point-to-point = a direct connection between two nodes."},
{title:"7. Simplex, Half-Duplex and Full-Duplex",tag:"Connection Types",summary:"These modes describe the direction and timing of data transmission.",definition:"Simplex allows only one node to send. Half-duplex allows both devices to send, but one at a time. Full-duplex allows both devices to send at the same time.",points:["Simplex: one-way communication only.","Half-duplex: two-way communication, but not simultaneously.","Full-duplex: two-way communication simultaneously."],remember:"Simplex = one direction; Half = take turns; Full = at the same time."},
{title:"8. Mesh Topology",tag:"Topologies",summary:"In a fully connected mesh, every device has a direct physical connection to every other device.",definition:"A fully connected mesh with n devices has n(n − 1) / 2 physical channels.",points:["Provides direct links between device pairs.","Disadvantages: difficult installation and reconfiguration.","Requires extensive wiring and can be expensive."],remember:"Mesh: many direct links, high cabling cost. Formula: n(n−1)/2."},
{title:"9. Star Topology",tag:"Topologies",summary:"All devices connect through a central controller or device.",definition:"Star topology is a network arrangement in which devices connect to a central controller.",points:["Advantages: less expensive than mesh and easy to install.","If one link fails, only that link is affected.","Fault identification and isolation are relatively easy.","Disadvantage: failure of the central controller can bring down the entire network.","Requires more cabling than ring and tree in the lecture comparison."],remember:"Star = central point. Central device failure affects the whole network."},
{title:"10. Bus Topology",tag:"Topologies",summary:"One long backbone cable links all devices; devices attach through drop lines and taps.",definition:"A bus topology is a multipoint configuration in which one long cable acts as a backbone to link all devices.",points:["Advantages: easy installation and less cabling than mesh, star and tree.","The backbone can follow an efficient path.","Disadvantages: difficult reconfiguration and fault isolation.","A break in the bus cable stops all transmission."],remember:"Bus = one shared backbone. A backbone break can stop the network."},
{title:"11. Ring Topology",tag:"Topologies",summary:"Each device connects to the two devices beside it, and signals travel around the ring.",definition:"In ring topology, each device has a dedicated point-to-point line to the two neighboring devices; a signal passes from device to device in one direction.",points:["Each device incorporates a repeater to regenerate bits.","Relatively easy to install and reconfigure.","Adding or deleting a device requires moving two connections.","A break can disable the entire network; the described flow is one-way."],remember:"Ring = neighbor-to-neighbor loop; a break may disable the ring."},
{title:"12. Tree and Hybrid Topologies",tag:"Topologies",summary:"Tree uses a hierarchical central trunk; hybrid combines different topology types.",definition:"A tree topology resembles a tree: one central node (the trunk) connects to other nodes through a single path. A hybrid topology combines multiple topologies as sub-networks.",points:["Tree: hierarchical structure with a central trunk.","Hybrid: different topology types are linked together.","The lecture example combines bus and ring departments through a central controller in a star."],remember:"Tree = hierarchy. Hybrid = combination."},
{title:"13. PAN and LAN",tag:"Network Categories",summary:"The slides classify networks by distance, including Personal Area Networks and Local Area Networks.",definition:"A PAN supports data transmission among personal devices such as computers, smartphones and tablets. A LAN interconnects computers within a limited area such as a home, school, laboratory, campus or office building.",points:["PAN focuses on an individual's personal devices.","A PAN may connect to a higher-level network through a gateway device.","LAN covers a limited geographical area.","Examples of LAN locations include homes, schools and offices."],remember:"PAN = personal devices. LAN = limited local area."},
{title:"14. Peer-to-Peer and Client-Server Networks",tag:"Network Categories",summary:"Peer-to-peer shares resources without a separate server; client-server uses clients and service-providing servers.",definition:"A P2P infrastructure allows two or more computer systems to connect and share resources without requiring a separate server or server software.",points:["P2P: connected systems share resources directly.","Client-server: clients request services from servers.","Servers may provide files, databases, web pages and shared resources."],remember:"P2P = no separate server required. Client-server = request/provider roles."},
{title:"15. Internet, Hosts and Protocols",tag:"Internet",summary:"The Internet is a network of interconnected networks; protocols govern communication.",definition:"A protocol defines the format and order of messages sent and received among network entities, and the actions taken on message transmission and receipt.",points:["Hosts (end systems) run network applications at the Internet edge.","Routers and switches forward packets.","Communication links may use fiber, copper, radio or satellite.","The Internet is often described as a network of networks.","Examples of protocols include HTTP, TCP, IP, Wi-Fi and Ethernet.","RFC means Request for Comments; IETF means Internet Engineering Task Force."],remember:"Protocol = message format + message order + actions."},
{title:"16. Network Core and Switching",tag:"Internet",summary:"The network core is a mesh of interconnected routers. Packet switching and circuit switching are two approaches.",definition:"In packet switching, hosts divide application messages into packets and routers forward them across links. In circuit switching, end-to-end resources are reserved for a call.",points:["Packet switching shares network resources and is suitable for bursty data.","Packets may experience queueing delay and loss during congestion.","Circuit switching reserves dedicated resources and offers circuit-like performance.","An unused reserved circuit can remain idle.","FDM allocates frequency bands; TDM allocates time slots."],remember:"Packet = shared/on demand. Circuit = reserved/dedicated."},
{title:"17. Delay, Loss and Throughput",tag:"Performance",summary:"Network performance is described using delay, packet loss and throughput.",definition:"Nodal delay is the sum of processing, queueing, transmission and propagation delays: d_nodal = d_proc + d_queue + d_trans + d_prop. Transmission delay is L/R; propagation delay is d/s.",points:["Processing: checking bit errors and selecting an output link.","Queueing: waiting at the output link; depends on congestion.","Transmission: time to push L bits onto a link at R bits/sec.","Propagation: time for a signal to travel distance d at speed s.","A packet is lost if it arrives when the buffer is full.","Throughput is the rate (bits/time unit) at which data is sent from sender to receiver.","The bottleneck link constrains end-to-end throughput."],remember:"Delay formula: proc + queue + trans + prop. Transmission = L/R. Propagation = d/s."},
{title:"18. Physical Media, Security and Layers",tag:"Media & Security",summary:"Physical media carry bits; security addresses attacks; protocol layers organize network functions.",definition:"Guided media carry signals through solid media such as copper and fiber. Unguided media carry signals freely, such as radio. The Internet protocol stack has five layers.",points:["Twisted pair: two insulated copper wires.","Coaxial cable: two concentric copper conductors; supports multiple frequency channels.","Fiber: glass fiber carries light pulses; high speed and low error rate; immune to electromagnetic noise.","Wireless radio: no physical wire; affected by reflection, obstruction and interference.","Security topics include malware, DoS, packet sniffing and IP spoofing.","Five layers: application, transport, network, link and physical.","Layering supports modularity, maintenance and updates."],remember:"Stack order: Application → Transport → Network → Link → Physical."}

,{title:"19. Internet: Nuts-and-Bolts View",tag:"Internet",summary:"The Internet connects billions of end systems through communication links and packet switches.",definition:"The Internet is a global system of interconnected networks. Hosts (end systems) run applications at the edge; routers and switches forward packets through interconnected networks.",points:["Hosts are also called end systems and include computers and other connected devices.","Packet switches forward packets (chunks of data); examples include routers and switches.","Communication links include fiber, copper, radio and satellite.","Transmission rate is also called bandwidth.","Networks are collections of devices, routers and links managed by an organization.","The Internet is a network of networks made from interconnected ISPs."],remember:"Hosts create/use data; links carry signals; packet switches forward packets."}
,{title:"20. Network Edge, Access Network and Network Core",tag:"Internet Structure",summary:"The Internet can be viewed as an edge, access networks and a core.",definition:"The network edge contains hosts (clients and servers). Access networks and physical media connect end systems to edge routers. The network core is a mesh of interconnected routers.",points:["Network edge: hosts, including clients and servers; servers are often in data centers.","Access networks connect end systems to edge routers.","Access networks may be residential, institutional or mobile.","Network core: interconnected routers and the network of networks.","The key access-network questions are transmission rate (bits/sec) and whether access is shared or dedicated."],remember:"Edge = hosts. Access = connection to edge router. Core = interconnected routers."}
,{title:"21. Cable-Based Access Network",tag:"Access Networks",summary:"Cable access uses a shared cable distribution network and frequency-separated services.",definition:"HFC (Hybrid Fiber Coax) is a network of cable and fiber that attaches homes to an ISP router. Homes share the access network to the cable headend.",points:["A cable modem connects the home to the cable access network; a splitter separates signals.","Data and TV are transmitted at different frequencies over the shared cable distribution network.","FDM (Frequency Division Multiplexing) sends different channels in different frequency bands.","The cable modem termination system (CMTS) is located at the cable headend.","The slide describes cable access as asymmetric: downstream and upstream rates differ.","The slide lists up to 40 Mbps–1.2 Gbps downstream and 30–100 Mbps upstream."],remember:"Cable = shared HFC network + FDM + cable headend/CMTS."}
,{title:"22. DSL Access Network",tag:"Access Networks",summary:"DSL uses an existing telephone line, separating voice and data by frequency.",definition:"Digital Subscriber Line (DSL) uses an existing telephone line to connect to a central office DSLAM. Voice goes to the telephone network and data goes to the Internet.",points:["A DSL modem connects the home through the telephone line.","A splitter separates voice and data signals.","Voice and data use different frequencies on a dedicated line to the central office.","DSLAM means DSL Access Multiplexer.","The slide lists 24–52 Mbps dedicated downstream and 3.5–16 Mbps dedicated upstream."],remember:"DSL = existing telephone line; voice + data at different frequencies; DSLAM at central office."}
,{title:"23. Home, Wireless and Enterprise Access",tag:"Access Networks",summary:"Home networks combine modems, routers, wired Ethernet and Wi-Fi; wireless and enterprise networks use different access technologies.",definition:"A wireless access network connects an end system to a router through a base station or access point. Enterprise networks combine wired and wireless links, switches and routers.",points:["A home network may include a cable/DSL modem, router, firewall, NAT, Ethernet and a Wi-Fi access point; these may be combined in one box.","WLANs (Wi-Fi, IEEE 802.11) typically cover a building or nearby area (about 100 ft in the slide).","Wide-area cellular access is provided by mobile operators and covers distances of tens of kilometers; the slide discusses 4G and 5G.","Enterprise networks in companies and universities combine wired and wireless technologies, switches and routers.","The slide lists Ethernet access rates of 100 Mbps, 1 Gbps and 10 Gbps."],remember:"Wi-Fi = local wireless access point. Cellular = wide-area mobile operator. Enterprise = mixed wired/wireless."}
,{title:"24. Host Packet Transmission and Transmission Delay",tag:"Performance",summary:"A host breaks application messages into packets and transmits each packet at the link rate.",definition:"A host sending function takes an application message, breaks it into packets of L bits, and transmits each packet into the access network at rate R bits/sec. Transmission delay is L/R seconds.",points:["L = packet length in bits.","R = link transmission rate in bits/sec; also called link capacity or bandwidth.","Transmission delay is the time needed to push all L bits into the link.","The slide example: L = 10 Kbits and R = 100 Mbps gives 0.1 ms transmission delay."],remember:"Transmission delay = packet size ÷ link rate = L/R."}
,{title:"25. Physical Media: Twisted Pair, Coax and Fiber",tag:"Physical Media",summary:"Guided media carry signals through solid materials, including copper and glass.",definition:"A physical link is the medium between transmitter and receiver. In guided media, signals propagate in solid media such as copper, fiber and coax.",points:["Twisted pair (TP): two insulated copper wires. The slide lists Category 5 at 100 Mbps/1 Gbps Ethernet and Category 6 at 10 Gbps Ethernet.","Coaxial cable: two concentric copper conductors; bidirectional; broadband with multiple frequency channels; hundreds of Mbps per channel.","Fiber optic cable: glass fiber carries light pulses, with each pulse representing a bit.","Fiber supports high-speed point-to-point transmission (tens to hundreds of Gbps), has a low error rate and is immune to electromagnetic noise."],remember:"Twisted pair = two copper wires. Coax = concentric conductors. Fiber = light in glass."}
,{title:"26. Wireless Radio and Satellite Media",tag:"Physical Media",summary:"Unguided media carry signals freely through the electromagnetic spectrum.",definition:"Wireless radio carries a signal in the electromagnetic spectrum without a physical wire. It is broadcast and, in the slide's description, half-duplex.",points:["Wireless propagation can be affected by reflection, obstruction by objects and interference.","Radio link types listed: terrestrial microwave, wireless LAN (Wi-Fi), wide-area cellular and satellite.","The slide lists terrestrial microwave channels up to 45 Mbps; satellite channels up to 45 Mbps.","The slide notes approximately 270 ms end-to-end delay for satellite and contrasts geosynchronous with low-Earth-orbit satellites."],remember:"Wireless = no wire; watch for reflection, obstruction and interference."}
,{title:"27. Packet Switching: Store-and-Forward",tag:"Network Core",summary:"A router must receive the entire packet before forwarding it on the next link.",definition:"In store-and-forward packet switching, the entire packet must arrive at a router before it can be transmitted on the next link. Transmission delay per link is L/R.",points:["For a packet of L bits on a link with rate R bps, transmission delay is L/R seconds.","For two links with equal rate and zero propagation delay, the example end-to-end transmission delay is 2L/R.","Slide example: L = 10 Kbits and R = 100 Mbps gives 0.1 ms for one-hop transmission delay."],remember:"Store-and-forward = receive the whole packet, then forward it."}
,{title:"28. Queueing Delay, Buffering and Packet Loss",tag:"Performance",summary:"Packets wait in router buffers when traffic competes for an output link; a full buffer causes loss.",definition:"Queueing delay is the time a packet waits at an output link. If the arrival rate temporarily exceeds the link transmission rate, packets queue; if the buffer fills, arriving packets are dropped.",points:["A router buffer is a waiting area for packets.","Queueing delay depends on the router's congestion level.","A buffer has finite capacity.","A packet arriving at a full buffer is lost/dropped.","A lost packet may be retransmitted by the previous node, by the source end system, or not retransmitted."],remember:"Arrival rate > output capacity → queue. Full buffer → packet loss."}
,{title:"29. Traffic Intensity and Queueing",tag:"Performance",summary:"Traffic intensity helps indicate how heavily a link is loaded.",definition:"Traffic intensity is La/R, where L is packet length (bits), a is the average packet arrival rate (packets/sec), and R is link bandwidth (bits/sec).",points:["La/R approximately 0: average queueing delay is small.","La/R approaches 1: average queueing delay becomes large.","La/R greater than 1: work arrives faster than it can be serviced; average delay becomes infinite in the slide's model."],remember:"Low intensity = small queue. Near 1 = large queue. Above 1 = overload."}
,{title:"30. Traceroute and Real Internet Delay",tag:"Performance",summary:"Traceroute measures delays to routers along an end-to-end path.",definition:"Traceroute is a program that measures delay from a source to routers along the Internet path toward a destination.",points:["Traceroute sends three probe packets toward router i using a time-to-live (TTL) value of i.","Router i returns a response to the sender.","The sender measures the time between sending a probe and receiving the reply.","An asterisk (*) means no response was received; the probe may have been lost or the router may not reply."],remember:"Traceroute = hop-by-hop path and delay measurements using TTL probes."}
,{title:"31. Throughput and the Bottleneck Link",tag:"Performance",summary:"Throughput is the delivery rate; the slowest constraining link limits end-to-end throughput.",definition:"Throughput is the rate (bits per unit time) at which bits are sent from sender to receiver. It may be instantaneous or averaged over a longer period.",points:["Instantaneous throughput is measured at a given point in time.","Average throughput is measured over a longer period.","For a simple server-client path, end-to-end throughput is constrained by the smaller of the server access rate Rs and client access rate Rc.","In the network scenario shown, 10 connections share a backbone bottleneck link R; per-connection throughput is min(Rc, Rs, R/10)."],remember:"Throughput is limited by the bottleneck — the smallest capacity along the path."}
,{title:"32. Internet Structure: ISPs, Peering and IXP",tag:"Internet Structure",summary:"Access ISPs interconnect through regional and global networks, peering links and exchange points.",definition:"An Internet Exchange Point (IXP) is a meeting point where multiple ISPs can connect and/or peer. Peering is a direct connection between networks to exchange traffic.",points:["Access ISPs must interconnect so any two hosts can exchange packets.","Connecting every access ISP directly to every other access ISP does not scale (O(N²)).","Customer ISPs may pay provider ISPs for transit; payments can reflect traffic exchanged.","Nearby ISPs at the same level may peer, often settlement-free, to reduce payments to providers.","An IXP is typically a facility with switches where multiple ISPs connect and peer; it charges a fee to connected ISPs.","Regional ISPs can connect access networks to larger ISPs.","Tier-1 ISPs are large global transit ISPs at the center of the hierarchy.","Content-provider networks (for example, Google or Microsoft) may operate private networks and connect close to end users, including by peering at IXPs."],remember:"Access ISP → regional ISP → Tier-1/global ISP; peering/IXP gives direct interconnection."}
,{title:"33. Network Security: Malware, DoS, Sniffing and Spoofing",tag:"Security",summary:"The slides introduce common attacks and the basic meaning of each.",definition:"Network security studies how networks can be attacked, how to defend them, and how to design architectures that resist attacks.",points:["Virus: self-replicating infection through receiving/executing an object, such as an email attachment.","Worm: self-replicating infection by passively receiving an object that gets itself executed.","Spyware: may record keystrokes and websites visited, then upload information.","Botnet: infected hosts controlled together; can be used for spam or DDoS attacks.","DoS: overwhelms resources such as a server or bandwidth with bogus traffic, making them unavailable to legitimate users.","Packet sniffing: a promiscuous network interface reads/records packets passing over shared media such as Ethernet or wireless.","IP spoofing: sending a packet with a false source address."],remember:"Virus/worm/spyware = malware; DoS = overload; sniffing = capture packets; spoofing = fake source IP."}
,{title:"34. Protocol Layers, Service Models and Encapsulation",tag:"Layers",summary:"Layering organizes network functions; encapsulation adds layer-specific control information.",definition:"Each layer implements a service through its own actions and relies on services provided by the layer below. Encapsulation occurs as data moves down the protocol stack and receives headers/control information.",points:["Application: supports network applications; examples include IMAP, SMTP and HTTP.","Transport: process-to-process data transfer; examples include TCP and UDP.","Network: routes datagrams from source to destination; includes IP and routing protocols.","Link: transfers data between neighboring network elements; examples include Ethernet, 802.11 (Wi-Fi) and PPP.","Physical: carries bits over the medium.","The slide's encapsulation illustration shows application message data becoming a transport segment, a network datagram and a link-layer frame.","Routers and switches process different portions/layers as packets traverse the network."],remember:"Application → Transport → Network → Link → Physical. Data gains headers as it moves down."}
,{title:"35. Internet History Timeline",tag:"History",summary:"The Internet evolved from early packet-switching research into a global platform.",definition:"The lecture organizes Internet history into early packet switching (1961–1972), internetworking (1972–1980), protocol growth (1980–1990), Web commercialization (1990s–2000s) and widespread Internet use (2005–present).",points:["1961: Kleinrock's queueing theory showed the effectiveness of packet switching; 1964: Baran's packet-switching work for military networks.","1967: ARPAnet conceived; 1969: first ARPAnet node operational; 1972: public demo, NCP, first email program and 15 nodes.","1974: Cerf and Kahn proposed an architecture for interconnecting networks; 1976: Ethernet at Xerox PARC; 1979: ARPAnet had 200 nodes.","1983: TCP/IP deployment; 1983: DNS defined; 1985: FTP defined; 1988: TCP congestion control.","Early 1990s: ARPAnet decommissioned and the Web emerged; 1991: NSF lifted restrictions on commercial use of NSFnet; 1994: Mosaic, later Netscape.","2005–present: smartphones, broadband, Wi-Fi/4G/5G, social networks, content-provider networks and cloud services expanded Internet use."],remember:"Key dates: 1969 ARPAnet; 1974 internetworking; 1983 TCP/IP; early 1990s Web; 2005+ everywhere."}
];

const egyptianTranslations = {'1. What Is a Computer Network?': 'شبكة الكمبيوتر هي مجموعة أجهزة متوصلة ببعض، بتتواصل مع بعض وبتشارك الموارد باستخدام قواعد اتصال مشتركة.', '2. Components of a Computer Network': 'المحاضرة بتحدد مكونات الشبكة الأساسية: أجهزة الكمبيوتر، الكابلات أو وسائط الاتصال، كارت الشبكة NIC، السويتشات، وبرامج تشغيل الشبكة.', '3. Benefits of Computer Networks': 'الشبكات بتسهّل مشاركة الملفات والأجهزة والموارد، وبتساعد الأجهزة تتواصل وتتبادل البيانات.', '4. Server and Client': 'السيرفر جهاز أو برنامج بيقدّم خدمات أو موارد، والـ Client هو الجهاز أو البرنامج اللي بيطلب الخدمة دي.', '5. Switch and Router': 'السويتش بيربط أجهزة الشبكة المحلية وبيوصل البيانات للجهاز المقصود، أما الراوتر فبيربط شبكات مختلفة وبيوجّه الباكيتس بينها.', '6. Point-to-Point Connection': 'اتصال نقطة لنقطة يعني وجود وصلة مباشرة بين جهازين، والوصلة دي بتكون مخصصة للتواصل بين الطرفين.', '7. Simplex, Half-Duplex and Full-Duplex': 'Simplex اتصال في اتجاه واحد بس؛ Half-Duplex الاتجاهين لكن مش في نفس الوقت؛ Full-Duplex الاتجاهين في نفس الوقت.', '8. Mesh Topology': 'في الـ Mesh كل جهاز بيكون متوصل بعدة أجهزة تانية، وده بيوفر مسارات بديلة لو وصلة وقعت.', '9. Star Topology': 'في الـ Star كل الأجهزة بتتصل بجهاز مركزي زي السويتش، ولو الجهاز المركزي وقف الشبكة كلها ممكن تتأثر.', '10. Bus Topology': 'في الـ Bus كل الأجهزة بتشارك كابل رئيسي واحد، والبيانات بتتنقل على نفس الكابل.', '11. Ring Topology': 'في الـ Ring كل جهاز متوصل باللي قبله واللي بعده، وده بيكوّن شكل دايرة.', '12. Tree and Hybrid Topologies': 'الـ Tree بتجمع مجموعات شبكات في شكل هرمي، والـ Hybrid بتجمع أكتر من نوع Topology في شبكة واحدة.', '13. PAN and LAN': 'PAN شبكة شخصية صغيرة حوالين شخص، أما LAN فهي شبكة محلية بتغطي مكان محدود زي بيت أو مكتب أو مبنى.', '14. Peer-to-Peer and Client-Server Networks': 'في Peer-to-Peer الأجهزة بتشارك الموارد مباشرة مع بعض، أما Client-Server فالسيرفر بيقدّم الخدمات والـ Clients بتطلبها.', '15. Internet, Hosts and Protocols': 'الإنترنت شبكة عالمية من شبكات متوصلة ببعض. الـ Hosts هي الأجهزة الطرفية، والـ Protocols هي القواعد اللي بتنظم تبادل البيانات.', '16. Network Core and Switching': 'قلب الشبكة هو مجموعة راوترات مترابطة بتنقل الباكيتس بين الشبكات. Switching هي طريقة تمرير البيانات خلال الشبكة.', '17. Delay, Loss and Throughput': 'Delay هو وقت وصول البيانات، وLoss يعني فقدان باكيتس، وThroughput هو معدل نقل البيانات الفعلي.', '18. Physical Media, Security and Layers': 'وسائط الاتصال بتنقل الإشارات سلكيًا أو لاسلكيًا، وأمن الشبكات بيحميها من الهجمات، والـ Layers بتقسّم وظائف الاتصال لمستويات.', '19. Internet: Nuts-and-Bolts View': 'الإنترنت بيتكوّن من أجهزة طرفية وروابط اتصال وأجهزة بتوجّه الباكيتس زي الراوترات والسويتشات؛ والروابط ممكن تكون فايبر أو نحاس أو لاسلكي أو أقمار صناعية.', '20. Network Edge, Access Network and Network Core': 'الـ Edge فيه الأجهزة اللي بتستخدم الشبكة، والـ Access Network بتوصل الأجهزة براوتر الشبكة، والـ Core هو شبكة الراوترات المترابطة.', '21. Cable-Based Access Network': 'اتصال الكابل بيستخدم شبكة HFC مشتركة من فايبر وكابل، وFDM بيفصل القنوات حسب التردد، والـ CMTS موجود عند محطة الكابل.', '22. DSL Access Network': 'تقنية DSL بتستخدم خط التليفون الموجود، وبتفصل الصوت عن البيانات باستخدام ترددات مختلفة، وDSLAM موجود في السنترال.', '23. Home, Wireless and Enterprise Access': 'شبكة البيت ممكن تجمع مودم وراوتر وWi-Fi وEthernet. الشبكات اللاسلكية بتوصل الأجهزة بنقطة وصول، وشبكات الشركات غالبًا بتجمع السلكي واللاسلكي.', '24. Host Packet Transmission and Transmission Delay': 'الجهاز بيقسّم الرسالة لباكيتس طول كل واحدة L bit، ويبعتها على وصلة سرعتها R. زمن الإرسال Transmission Delay بيساوي L مقسومة على R.', '25. Physical Media: Twisted Pair, Coax and Fiber': 'Twisted Pair سلكين نحاس معزولين وملفوفين، وCoax كابل فيه موصلين نحاس متحدي المركز، وFiber بينقل البيانات في صورة نبضات ضوء داخل ألياف زجاجية.', '26. Wireless Radio and Satellite Media': 'الاتصال اللاسلكي بينقل الإشارة من غير سلك باستخدام موجات الراديو، وممكن يتأثر بالانعكاس أو العوائق أو التداخل. الاتصال بالأقمار الصناعية بيكون له تأخير ملحوظ.', '27. Packet Switching: Store-and-Forward': 'في Store-and-Forward الراوتر لازم يستقبل الباكيت كاملة الأول، وبعد كده يبدأ يبعتها على الوصلة اللي بعدها.', '28. Queueing Delay, Buffering and Packet Loss': 'لما الباكيتس توصل أسرع من قدرة الوصلة على إرسالها، بتستنى في الـ Buffer. ولو الـ Buffer اتملى، الباكيتس الجديدة ممكن تضيع.', '29. Traffic Intensity and Queueing': 'Traffic Intensity بتقيس مدى انشغال الوصلة باستخدام La/R. لما القيمة تقرب من 1، وقت الانتظار بيزيد جدًا، ولو أكبر من 1 بيكون الحمل أعلى من قدرة الوصلة.', '30. Traceroute and Real Internet Delay': 'Traceroute بتقيس التأخير من المصدر لحد الراوترات المختلفة في الطريق، وبتساعدنا نفهم المسار ومصادر التأخير.', '31. Throughput and the Bottleneck Link': 'Throughput هو معدل نقل البيانات الفعلي من المصدر للوجهة، وغالبًا أبطأ وصلة في المسار هي اللي بتحدد السرعة القصوى.', '32. Internet Structure: ISPs, Peering and IXP': 'الإنترنت مكوّن من شبكات ومزودي خدمة ISP مترابطين. Peering هو اتفاق لتبادل الترافيك، وIXP مكان بتتصل فيه شبكات مختلفة ببعض.', '33. Network Security: Malware, DoS, Sniffing and Spoofing': 'Malware برامج ضارة، وDoS هجوم بيعطّل الخدمة بإغراقها بالطلبات، وSniffing اعتراض أو مراقبة البيانات، وIP Spoofing تزوير عنوان IP.', '34. Protocol Layers, Service Models and Encapsulation': 'الـ Protocol Stack بيقسّم الاتصال لخمس طبقات: Application وTransport وNetwork وLink وPhysical. Encapsulation يعني كل طبقة بتضيف معلومات تحكم للبيانات قبل إرسالها.', '35. Internet History Timeline': 'تاريخ الإنترنت بدأ بأبحاث تبديل الحزم، وبعدها ARPAnet، ثم TCP/IP، وبعدها الويب، ومع الوقت انتشر الإنترنت السريع والموبايلات والسحابة.'};
